Looking at this migration, the core issue is attempting a **column rename while the column is in use** - a destructive change that requires a multi-phase migration strategy.

The safe approach is an **expand-contract pattern**:
- **Expand phase** (this migration): Add the new column alongside the old one
- **Application deployment**: Update code to use `display_name` 
- **Contract phase** (future migration): Drop `full_name` after the app no longer references it

```json
{
  "rewrite_sql": "-- Prevent blocking on lock acquisition
SET lock_timeout = '2s';
-- Allow time for batched backfill
SET statement_timeout = '300s';

-- Add new column (fast, metadata-only)
ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN display_name text;

-- Backfill existing data in batches to minimize lock contention
DO $$
DECLARE
    batch_size INTEGER := 5000;
    rows_updated INTEGER;
BEGIN
    LOOP
        UPDATE users
        SET display_name = full_name
        WHERE id IN (
            SELECT id 
            FROM users 
            WHERE display_name IS NULL 
            LIMIT batch_size
        );

        GET DIAGNOSTICS rows_updated = ROW_COUNT;
        EXIT WHEN rows_updated = 0;

        -- Small delay between batches
        PERFORM pg_sleep(0.05);
    END LOOP;
END $$;",
  "notes": "Changed from a direct RENAME (which takes ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock and breaks running code) to a safe two-phase migration. This expand phase: (1) adds lock_timeout to fail fast instead of queueing behind production traffic, (2) adds the display_name column (metadata-only, very fast), (3) backfills data in 5000-row batches to minimize lock contention. Both full_name and display_name will coexist. The contract phase - dropping full_name - is deferred to a later migration that ships AFTER the application is deployed and no longer references the old column name.",
  "deferred": true
}
```
